(load-lain "lib/context-policy.lain")
(define role-cell "Construct executable Lain in a live lexical session. Return complete raw Lain source only. Use history only when supplied in working-evidence; an empty history means earlier details are omitted. Select any missing historical evidence by querying. If an interface or rule is unknown, emit a retrieval reply now instead of guessing or spending the reply speculating. When a suitable live callable exists under the current assumptions, resolve and apply it to compute the delivered output. Rewriting its arithmetic from archived source does not satisfy reuse. Keep its catalog id and interface in your selected working-evidence across retrieval rounds. Create a new callable only when the existing computation cannot express the changed rules. A catalog claim is not measured truth. Never guess that empty search proves absence. Do not invent tool names or precomputed answers.")
(define language-introduction-cell "Lain declarations build relationships, not a host execution sequence. (define add-one (network [x] (+ x 1))) defines a callable; (add-one 4) produces 5. (define offset 3) then (define shift (network [x] (+ x offset))) captures the lexical offset. Applying shift later reuses its declaration and captured environment. min and max choose numeric bounds. (round-money 1.235M) gives 1.24M, half up; use it only on final totals. Parameters can remain unknown: their dependent outputs wait, so partial evaluation preserves connected topology. (if (> x 0) x (- 0 x)) selects a branch. A network returns its final expression. (let [a 2 b (+ a 3)] (* a b)) is 10. (-> value stage-result) connects the existing output; do not define/shadow stage-result. (data:record :key value) builds a record; (data:get :key record) reads it. (seq:of x y) is a vector; (data:get 0 xs) reads its first element. (cell:reference shift) exposes a live reference; (cell:value reference) observes its callable, which can then be applied. Storage effects publish receipts after commit; use (cell:first receipt) before projecting fields from a completed one-shot receipt; use their values as dependencies. when gates availability, not boolean truth; use if for choices. Extending this lexical child preserves earlier admitted declarations. A correction must use fresh names or connect existing outputs, not redefine conflicting bindings. Documentation examples teach operations and are not installed bindings.")
(define context-guide "The following operations are available. context:query takes an EDN query string, EDN rules string, nil or previous :next cursor, and page limit 1..32. Example: (context:query \"[:find ?id ?description :in $ % :where [?e :catalog/id ?id] [?e :catalog/description ?description]]\" \"[]\" nil 8). Returns :rows, :basis, :next, :complete?, :absence-established? false. Query only triples and named rules with :in $ %. Schema: :catalog/id unique keyword; :catalog/description, :catalog/interface, :catalog/assumptions, :catalog/example strings; :catalog/status, :catalog/tag, :catalog/author, :catalog/evidence-kind keywords. :catalog/evidence, :catalog/depends-on, :catalog/capability, :catalog/supersedes are entity references. To retrieve evidence ids, join [?c :catalog/evidence ?e] [?e :catalog/id ?id]. Agent claims have author :agent; raw observed artifacts have :observation, which does not certify their text. (context:read :evidence/example 0 1024) returns exact :text, codepoint :next offset or nil, :complete?. Read more chunks explicitly. (context:resolve :operator/example-v1) returns a live cell reference or an error; ((cell:value reference) 4) applies the resolved callable. (context:inspect reference nil 8) returns immediate :value, :edges, :next; depth is zero, neighboring references may be inspected explicitly. Inspection :value for a callable contains :argument-count, :parameters, :declaration and :captured-environment. Resolve returns the reference itself, not a record containing it. Missing live references are reported; there is no automatic restore. context:register takes metadata EDN string and cell:reference of a callable, returning :registered. Required fields: :catalog/id, :catalog/description, :catalog/interface, :catalog/assumptions. Choose your own versioned id. context:index takes an EDN claim string with id, description, and nonempty :catalog/evidence lookup refs (vectors, never data:record values), e.g. [[:catalog/id :evidence/example]]. It returns :indexed or :error. Attach evidence to the existing callable identity using its original description; never change an existing identity's metadata. New assumptions require explicit new ids and :catalog/supersedes lookup refs. Preserve failed/superseded evidence. Registration is not a proof. Existing task artifacts are listed in working-evidence at indexing. context:text renders a value as EDN for constructing metadata strings. The prompt is limited to 12000 input tokens including template; select concise working evidence and keep omitted details accessible by id.")
(define output-contract "Every reply must connect exactly one complete record to the existing stage-result port. For stage :work, either retrieve with (-> (data:record :decision :retrieve :evidence (seq:of actual-query-result actual-read-result)) stage-result), deliver with (-> (data:record :decision :done :answer computed-number :reply (str \"Quote: \" computed-number)) stage-result), or report :decision :unresolved and :reason text. Retrieval results must be real operation values, not invented summaries. At most four retrieval rounds followed by execution; one diagnostic-driven correction per failed stage. A retrieval reply ends this request without solving the task. Its :evidence value becomes working-evidence in the next request. This replaces the previous working-evidence; carry forward the small pieces you still need. Retrieved query rows are vectors, not records: (data:get 0 (data:get 0 (data:get :rows page))) selects column 0 of row 0 after checking the result. Results of operations in this reply are not visible to your reasoning until you return :retrieve. Retrieve the interface and assumptions before applying an unfamiliar callable; match all arguments in their documented order. Use another round to read supporting evidence when claims alone are insufficient. Search may precede execution within a reply only when the necessary interface is already known. Construct a reusable parametric callable when requested, register its live reference, and make delivery depend on registration receipt. For stage :index, inspect working-evidence (source, artifact ids, outcome and validation), describe problems, operators, interfaces, assumptions, dependencies and examples with evidence references; use fresh explicit versions for changes. Connect (data:record :decision :indexed :receipts (seq:of receipt1 receipt2)) to stage-result, where each receipt is from context:index. Do not solve a new task during indexing. Failed tasks still need evidence-backed failure descriptions. The examples are output shapes, not task solutions.")
(define retrieval-example-cell "Complete standalone retrieval reply example (choose your own query; no example bindings are installed):\n(define lookup-page (cell:first (context:query \"[:find ?id ?description :in $ % :where [?e :catalog/id ?id] [?e :catalog/description ?description]]\" \"[]\" nil 8)))\n(-> (data:record :decision :retrieve :evidence lookup-page) stage-result)")
(define context-seed
 (let [e0 (empty-env)
       e1 (context:bind e0 "role-cell" (cell:reference role-cell))
       e2 (context:bind e1 "language-introduction-cell" (cell:reference language-introduction-cell))
       e3 (context:bind e2 "context-guide" (cell:reference context-guide))
       e4 (context:bind e3 "output-contract" (cell:reference output-contract))
       e5 (context:bind e4 "+" (cell:reference +))
       e6 (context:bind e5 "-" (cell:reference -))
       e7 (context:bind e6 "*" (cell:reference *))
       e8 (context:bind e7 "/" (cell:reference /))
       e9 (context:bind e8 "<" (cell:reference <))
       e10 (context:bind e9 "<=" (cell:reference <=))
       e11 (context:bind e10 ">" (cell:reference >))
       e12 (context:bind e11 ">=" (cell:reference >=))
       e13 (context:bind e12 "=" (cell:reference =))
       e14 (context:bind e13 "str" (cell:reference str))
       e15 (context:bind e14 "data:record" (cell:reference data:record))
       e16 (context:bind e15 "data:get" (cell:reference data:get))
       e17 (context:bind e16 "data:equal?" (cell:reference data:equal?))
       e18 (context:bind e17 "seq:of" (cell:reference seq:of))
       e19 (context:bind e18 "cell:reference" (cell:reference cell:reference))
       e20 (context:bind e19 "cell:value" (cell:reference cell:value))
       e21 (context:bind e20 "context:query" (cell:reference context:query))
       e22 (context:bind e21 "context:read" (cell:reference context:read))
       e23 (context:bind e22 "context:inspect" (cell:reference context:inspect))
       e24 (context:bind e23 "context:resolve" (cell:reference context:resolve))
       e25 (context:bind e24 "context:register" (cell:reference context:register))
       e26 (context:bind e25 "context:index" (cell:reference context:index))
       e27 (context:bind e26 "context:text" (cell:reference context:text))
             e28 (context:bind e27 "cell:first" (cell:reference cell:first))
      e29 (context:bind e28 "retrieval-example-cell" (cell:reference retrieval-example-cell))
      ] e29))

(define pricing-context-seed
 (context:bind (context:bind (context:bind context-seed "round-money" (cell:reference round-money))
                            "min" (cell:reference min)) "max" (cell:reference max)))
